  held-out R2 clearly above the shuffled control, and the top of f populated
  by mutations known to matter
      -> block growth is predictable from block contents. A shared f can give
         a rate to a block that has not been seen growing, which is the one
         thing reweighting existing blocks cannot do. The joint model is worth
         building.
  held-out R2 at or below the shuffled control
      -> growth is not a function of composition at this granularity, and
         coupling pi to theta this way will not help. Worth an afternoon to
         have ruled out.

  Caveat that no R2 removes: composition and growth are both read off the same
  fitted blocks, so f is a statement about this partition, not about mutations
  in the abstract. A block that grew is partly WHY the model made that block.

